WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.221
Matryoshka embeddings: shorter vectors on purpose.

2
00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:23.875
Matryoshka Representation Learning (Kusupati and colleagues, 2022) trains a model so that the leading coordinates of its vector are themselves a good embedding: the first 256 dimensions work as a coarse representation, the first 512 as a finer one, and so on, like nested dolls.

3
00:00:24.155 --> 00:00:34.736
The paper reports up to 14 times smaller embeddings at the same accuracy on an image classification benchmark, and corresponding speedups for large-scale retrieval.

4
00:00:35.016 --> 00:00:37.000
You will meet this as a size option.

5
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:45.451
OpenAI’s dimensions parameter, Voyage’s output_dimension, Gemini’s output_dimensionality, and Nomic Embed v1.

6
00:00:45.731 --> 00:00:48.653
5’s documented dimensions all rely on it.

7
00:00:48.933 --> 00:01:03.248
OpenAI’s guide gives an example where text-embedding-3-large cut to 256 dimensions still beat the older text-embedding-ada-002 at its full 1,536 on MTEB.

8
00:01:03.528 --> 00:01:04.872
Two practical rules.

9
00:01:05.152 --> 00:01:17.781
First, renormalize after truncating: a prefix of a unit vector is shorter than 1, so the dot product stops equaling cosine (the Predict question in this lesson’s knowledge check shows the arithmetic).

10
00:01:18.061 --> 00:01:27.981
Asking the API for the smaller size avoids the problem; if you truncate stored vectors yourself, both OpenAI and Voyage tell you to normalize again.

11
00:01:28.261 --> 00:01:34.448
Second, measure the loss on your data: run your labeled set at full size and at the truncated size.

12
00:01:34.728 --> 00:01:39.272
If recall@10 barely moves, you have just cut your storage by a factor of four.
